## Summary

Set out to cut the wakes `committedTriggers` (`internal/db/trigger.go`) deliver to every worker watching a table, whatever columns changed: the five document workers (fetch, extract, classify, digest, sweep) each rescan the whole `DOCUMENT`/`CONTENT` table on every commit. The proposed fix was declaring the columns each inbox cares about on its trigger, measured first.

Measured on a production snapshot (`tools/snapshot`) rather than with production logs, using a temporary test file that was deleted afterwards. Each worker's pending scan was timed on 60k `DOCUMENT` / 35k `CONTENT` rows: sweep 22.8 ms, extract 4.4, fetch 1.8, classify 1.5, digest 1.5, 32 ms for all five. Real commits per hour came from the snapshot's `CHANGES` tabs (commits keyed by second and actor). The busiest hours were classify re-classifying mail (3,125 commits/hour) and import/extract backfills (3,900 commits/hour). They spend about 95–125 s scanning, 2.6–3.5% of one core, all in background workers off the request path. Normal hours spend seconds.

Decision (ian agreed): not worth the machinery. Triggers are unchanged. The `: pending` took logs added during the work were removed, since in production they'd write five lines per document commit. The TODO item is deleted. The branch's only net change is the TODO removal. Column filters would cut the busiest hour from about 95 s to about 10 s of scanning, so revisit only if sweep's scan grows a lot.

